In article <1411@yenta.alb.nm.us> yenta!dt@bbx.basis.com writes:
>swarren@convex.com (Steve Warren) writes:
>
>>o Avoids cheapening the game with confirmations, while accomplishing much
>>  the same effect when moving normally - "you stop to avoid the pool"
>
>I would much rather have it treat pools and monsters as walls when moving
>non-aggresively, i.e. no message and no wasted turn.  After all, a monster
>blocking your way is a lot like a wall.  As long as it's there, you can't
>walk that way.  Same with a pool.  You can attack, or swim, but not walk.

Well, if you mis-key by attempting to walk into a pool or a monster, you
have made a mistake.  I personally don't think that this should be a fatal
mistake (unless the monster is really tough and you are not).  But it *is*
a mistake.

Think about the situation - you have started to walk forward, but you've
noticed that you are about to step into a pool, so you pull up short, maybe
standing on tip-toe, windmilling your arms to keep your balance at the edge of
the pool.  "You stop to avoid the pool."  While you are distracted trying to
stay out of the water, you are going to get smacked by any monsters standing
near.  I think that is only fair, and it will tend to enforce respect for
pools.

